{
	title: 'Hal knows its version; ctrl-r hint for new code; host ≠ peer notice',
	description: "Each Hal process records the version it loaded at start: its checkout's HEAD (short hash) plus '+dirty' when the tree had uncommitted changes, found off the startup path (after the first frame). It is in the diag log and in /version, for bug reports. New code: every ~10 s each process asks git for its own checkout's HEAD (git rev-parse, a few ms); when it differs from the loaded HEAD, the help row gains 'ctrl-r: load new code'. Only commits count: agents commit after ./test passes and a pull lands finished code, so uncommitted edits (possibly half-written) never offer a restart. Host ≠ peer: the host sends its version with snapshots; a peer whose version differs shows it in the notice row, e.g. 'host runs abcd123, this peer bcd4321'; the host has no such notice. No remote restart: /restart and Ctrl-R restart only the process they are pressed in. Tests: a commit after start adds the hint, an uncommitted edit does not; differing host and peer versions produce the notice, equal ones none.",
	status: 'planned',
	needs: []
}
